    {
      "math_academy_id": "q-221409",
      "knowledge_point_id": "34299c34-b199-5100-8fb6-c190eb1db2f7",
      "knowledge_point": "Completing the Square When the Perfect Square Is Isolated",
      "problem": "Rewrite the following expression as the sum of a perfect square and a constant.\n\n$({x}^{2}-17x+{(\\frac{17}{2})}^{2})+5=(x\\,\\,-$\n{{field-1}} $)^{2}+$\n{{field-2}}",
      "worked_solution": "First, we recall the following formula:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-2ax+{a}^{2}=(x-a)^{2}\n$$\n\nNotice that we have a perfect square in the parentheses that we can factor as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-17x+{(\\frac{17}{2})}^{2} & = \\\\ {x}^{2}-2\\cdot \\frac{17}{2}x+{(\\frac{17}{2})}^{2} & = \\\\ {(x-\\frac{17}{2})}^{2} & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({x}^{2}-17x+{(\\frac{17}{2})}^{2})+5 & = \\\\ {(x-\\frac{17}{2})}^{2}+5 & .\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "answer_fields": [
        {
