      "knowledge_point": "Completing the Square When the Perfect Square Is Isolated",
      "problem": "Rewrite the following expression as the difference of a perfect square and a constant.\n\n$({x}^{2}-11x+\\frac{121}{4})-10=(x\\,\\,-$\n{{field-1}} $)^{2}-$\n{{field-2}}",
      "worked_solution": "First, we recall the following formula:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-2ax+{a}^{2}=(x-a)^{2}\n$$\n\nNotice that we have a perfect square in the parentheses that we can factor as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-11x+\\frac{121}{4} & = \\\\ {x}^{2}-11x+{(\\frac{11}{2})}^{2} & = \\\\ {x}^{2}-2\\cdot \\frac{11}{2}x+{(\\frac{11}{2})}^{2} & = \\\\ {(x-\\frac{11}{2})}^{2} & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({x}^{2}-11x+\\frac{121}{4})-10 & = \\\\ {(x-\\frac{11}{2})}^{2}-10 & .\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "answer_fields": [
