      "math_academy_id": "e-15019",
      "knowledge_point_id": "34299c34-b199-5100-8fb6-c190eb1db2f7",
      "knowledge_point": "Completing the Square When the Perfect Square Is Isolated",
      "problem": "Rewrite the expression \n\n$$\n\\left(x^2 + 7x + \\dfrac{49}{4}\\right) + 3\n$$\n\nas the sum of a perfect square and a constant.",
      "worked_solution": "First, we recall the following formula:\n\n$$\nx^2 + {\\color{red}{2}}{\\color{blue}{a}}x + a^2 = (x + {\\color{blue}{a}})^2\n$$\n\nNotice that we have a perfect square in the parentheses that we can factor as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+7x+\\frac{49}{4} & = \\\\ {x}^{2}+7x+{(\\frac{7}{2})}^{2} & = \\\\ {x}^{2}+2\\cdot \\frac{7}{2}x+{(\\frac{7}{2})}^{2} & = \\\\ {(x+\\frac{7}{2})}^{2} & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({x}^{2}+7x+\\frac{49}{4})+3 & = \\\\ {(x+\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}+3 & .\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "difficulty": null,
      "answer_fields": [],
      "missing_source_fields": [
        "difficulty",
        "answer_fields"
      ]
    },
    {
      "math_academy_id": "e-15020",
      "knowledge_point_id": "ec6076ff-1538-5bc0-973d-33d908b0b4d2",
      "knowledge_point": "Completing the Square on a Quadratic Expression",
      "problem": "Complete the square on the quadratic expression $x^2 + 7x - 1.$",
      "worked_solution": "First, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with ${\\color{red}{2}}$ as a factor:\n\n$$\nx^2 + {\\color{red}{2}}\\cdot {\\color{blue}{\\dfrac{7}{2}}}x - {\\color{purple}{1}}\n$$\n\nNow, here's the trick. We *add* and *then subtract* $\\left({\\color{blue}{\\dfrac{7}{2}}}\\right)^2,$ writing the constant term ${\\color{purple}{-1}}$ last:\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{x^2 + {\\color{red}{2}}\\cdot {\\color{blue}{\\dfrac{7}{2}}}x  + \\left({\\color{blue}{\\dfrac{7}{2}}}\\right)^2} - \\left({\\color{blue}{\\dfrac{7}{2}}}\\right)^2 - {\\color{purple}{1}}\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n\\left(x + {\\color{blue}{\\dfrac{7}{2}}}\\right)^2  - \\left({\\color{blue}{\\dfrac{7}{2}}}\\right)^2 - {\\color{purple}{1}}\n$$\n\nFinally, evaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n\\left(x + {\\color{blue}{\\dfrac{7}{2}}}\\right)^2  - \\dfrac{53}{4}.\n$$",
      "difficulty": null,
      "answer_fields": [],
      "missing_source_fields": [
        "difficulty",
        "answer_fields"
      ]
    },
    {
      "math_academy_id": "e-15021",
      "knowledge_point_id": "1481d4a2-64b4-590a-9dbb-c39d3364bb59",
      "knowledge_point": "Expressions Containing a Negative Linear Term",
      "problem": "Complete the square on the quadratic expression $x^2 - 7x.$",
      "worked_solution": "First, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with ${\\color{red}{2}}$ as a factor:\n\n$$\nx^2 - {\\color{red}{2}}\\cdot {\\color{blue}{\\dfrac{7}{2}}}x\n$$\n\nNow, here's the trick. We *add* and *then subtract* $\\left({\\color{blue}{\\dfrac{7}{2}}}\\right)^2{:}$ \n\n$$\n\\underbrace{x^2 - {\\color{red}{2}}\\cdot {\\color{blue}{\\dfrac{7}{2}}}x + \\left({\\color{blue}{\\dfrac{7}{2}}}\\right)^2} - \\left({\\color{blue}{\\dfrac{7}{2}}}\\right)^2\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n\\left(x - {\\color{blue}{\\dfrac{7}{2}}}\\right)^2  - \\left({\\color{blue}{\\dfrac{7}{2}}}\\right)^2\n$$\n\nFinally, evaluating the last term, we get\n\n$$\n\\left(x - {\\color{blue}{\\dfrac{7}{2}}}\\right)^2  - \\dfrac{49}{4}.\n$$",
